Okay, here's an example of how to generate a table of contents automatically in LaTeX, along with customization options:

**Basic Table of Contents**

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction.

\subsection{Background}
Some background information.

\section{Methods}
Here are the methods used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Details on data collection.

\end{document}
```

**Explanation:**

*   `\tableofcontents`: This single command generates the table of contents.  It automatically detects sections, subsections, subsubsections, etc., and creates the entries.

**Customizing the Table of Contents**

You can customize the appearance of the table of contents using the `\setcounter{tocdepth}{<level>}` command.  The `tocdepth` level determines how many levels of headings are included.

*   `\setcounter{tocdepth}{1}`: Includes sections and subsections. (Default)
*   `\setcounter{tocdepth}{2}`: Includes sections, subsections, and subsubsections.
*   `\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}`: Includes sections, subsections, subsubsections, and subsubsubsection.

**Example with Custom Depth:**

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\setcounter{tocdepth}{2} % Include sections and subsections

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction.

\subsection{Background}
Some background information.

\subsubsection{Further Details}
More specific details.

\section{Methods}
Here are the methods used.

\subsection{Data Collection}
Details on data collection.

\end{document}
```

**Formatting Options (using `\addcontentsline`)**

For more fine-grained control, you can use `\addcontentsline` to manually add entries to the table of contents.  This is useful for things like appendices or figures that aren't automatically detected.

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\begin{document}

\setcounter{tocdepth}{2}

\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction.

\subsection{Background}
Some background information.

\appendix
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Appendix A} % Add Appendix A to the TOC
\section{Appendix A}
Some content in the appendix.

\end{document}
```

**Explanation of `\addcontentsline`:**

*   `\addcontentsline{toc}{<level>}{<text>}`
    *   `toc`:  Specifies that you're adding to the table of contents.
    *   `<level>`: The level of the heading (e.g., `section`, `subsection`, `chapter`).
    *   `<text>`: The text to appear in the table of contents.

